WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:07.680
Speaker 1: We have a headline that is scaring many&nbsp;
in our audience. 1 million voters have switched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.960
to the Republican Party. Does this spell disaster&nbsp;
for the upcoming 2022 midterms? Well, let's think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.680
about it. These are the most important midterms&nbsp;
in a long time. Now, if you've been watching this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.480
show for a while, you know, I am not hyperbolic&nbsp;
with the importance of any particular election.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.680
I've said this before. In 2012, when Mitt Romney&nbsp;
was challenging Barack Obama's reelection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.200
I supported Barack Obama. I didn't support Mitt&nbsp;
Romney. I believed Obama was the better choice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.120
But there was no feeling, at least for me, that&nbsp;
the future of the country was riding on that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.360
Yeah, I agreed more with Obama than Romney. Romney&nbsp;
would have lowered taxes for the rich and would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.960
have done other things I don't agree with in&nbsp;
his social views. As a conservative Mormon or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:55.600
not my views. But it was not the most important&nbsp;
election of our time of the 2014 midterm. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.600
not the most important election of our&nbsp;
time, and I didn't call it that at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.920
I can't remember a more important midterm than&nbsp;
the 2022 midterm. And so when we now see these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:11.120
headlines, more than 1 million voters switched to&nbsp;
the Republican Party in a warning for Democrats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:16.720
it is a concerning and disturbing headline.&nbsp;
Now, yesterday, we talked about on the one hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:23.440
it seems as though Democrats actually have some&nbsp;
pretty reasonable they have a reasonable case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:30.000
to make with two issues the overturning of Roe v&nbsp;
Wade and the unwillingness and disinterest from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.120
Republicans in acting on gun safety legislation.&nbsp;
Democrats have some powerful things to run on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.520
If voters still care about them in four and a&nbsp;
half months, that's good for Democrats. On the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.640
other hand, what about this? A political shift&nbsp;
is beginning to take hold, writes the Associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.520
Press. Tens of thousands of suburban swing voters&nbsp;
who helped fuel the Democratic Party's gains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:56.080
are becoming Republicans. More than a million&nbsp;
voters across 43 states have switched to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:01.600
Republican Party over the last year, according to&nbsp;
data analyzed by the AP. The previously unreported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.640
number reflects a phenomenon playing out&nbsp;
in virtually every region of the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.360
Democratic and Republican states, along with&nbsp;
cities and small towns. In the period since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.640
Joe Biden replaced Trump. Nowhere is the shift&nbsp;
more pronounced and dangerous for Democrats than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.360
in the suburbs where well-educated swing voters&nbsp;
who turned against Trump's Republican Party in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:27.920
recent years are swinging back. Swinging back. Far&nbsp;
more people are switching to the Republican Party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:32.720
in counties from Denver to Atlanta, Pittsburgh&nbsp;
and Cleveland. Republicans also gaining ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.840
in. Medium sized cities such as Harrisburg,&nbsp;
Pennsylvania. Raleigh, North Carolina. Augusta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:45.760
Georgia. And Des Moines, Iowa. Now, what does this&nbsp;
mean? Well, on the one hand, we have to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:51.040
that the United States is a country plagued by&nbsp;
ignorance. The country is plagued by ignorance. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:57.520
you see very clear differences. Democrats,&nbsp;
not nearly liberal or left wing enough for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:03.440
but at least connected to reality on fundamental&nbsp;
issues of science, of reality to some degree about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:07.440
economic justice. And it's not a perfect party.&nbsp;
Of course, I have so many complaints about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:12.000
Democratic Party, but at least connected to&nbsp;
reality. On the Republican side, particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.680
as Trumpism has has taken over, there has been&nbsp;
this plague of ignorance which is not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.080
present, it's celebrated. And you'll see some&nbsp;
clips later in the show that remind us of this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:30.320
And it is not a shock in that sense that we&nbsp;
are seeing voters to some degree fall for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.400
Joe Biden. Did high gas prices,&nbsp;
Joe Biden did inflation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.840
Joe Biden is responsible for what's happening&nbsp;
in Ukraine or whatever the case may be. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.360
that they would say, well, I guess I'll abandon&nbsp;
the Democratic Party and vote for Republicans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:47.200
It doesn't make any sense when we think about&nbsp;
it. It seems completely backwards. But that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.080
not a shock. But there's a couple of caveats&nbsp;
with this voter registration shift that I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:57.440
are important to keep in mind. One is there are&nbsp;
many libertarians that are becoming Republicans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:04.640
And so there's a difference between a Democrat&nbsp;
becoming a Republican and a libertarian becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:09.760
a Republican. Libertarians in the United States&nbsp;
tend to vote with the Republican Party anyway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.280
The American libertarian movement is pretty right&nbsp;
wing. And so if you take this would sort of be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:21.600
like, I'm an independent voter, I'm unregistered,&nbsp;
I'm not a Democrat or a Republican. If I were to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:27.680
announce to you I am now becoming a Democrat, if&nbsp;
a million people like me said I am now a Democrat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.760
does it really change anything about the outcome&nbsp;
of elections? Because I mostly vote for Democrats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.560
anyway. I just don't care about the Democratic&nbsp;
Party. It's not if I'm going to be a member of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:42.160
something, I want to do that enthusiastically.&nbsp;
I have no enthusiasm for the Democratic Party as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.920
a party, so I'm just an independent and I vote&nbsp;
for Democrats. 4 million people like me become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:52.000
Democrats doesn't really make a difference. And so&nbsp;
of those million, I don't have the exact numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.200
but a bunch of them are libertarians who are&nbsp;
mostly right wing and vote for Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:02.000
or don't vote at all becoming Republicans. And it&nbsp;
doesn't necessarily mean that the balance is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.440
to shift. The other caveat is that this study&nbsp;
about the million voters becoming Republicans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:12.160
This was before the gun rights discussion that&nbsp;
has surfaced after a number of school shootings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:16.880
and mass shootings. And this is before&nbsp;
the Supreme Court overturned Roe v Wade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:22.880
And so while we can believe the data that the&nbsp;
data this is good data, that the data is real.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:27.520
There's the libertarian element that I mentioned,&nbsp;
which is a caveat. And there's also we don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:33.360
yet what the full effect of Roe v Wade being&nbsp;
overturned and the anger about Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.240
unwillingness to deal with with gun safety,&nbsp;
that is not yet part of the polling. So is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:44.240
a good sign for 22? No, it's not a good sign,&nbsp;
but it doesn't have to be the disastrous sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.720
that some are making it out to be. That's my&nbsp;
interpretation. Let me know yours. You can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:53.520
find me on Twitter at David Pakman. We're going to&nbsp;
take a quick break and be back right after this.

